{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention instead of marking each of its sites with `{impl}`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The text has one clause: the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill \"MUST direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention instead of marking each of its sites with `{impl}`.\" That clause is about what the skill document tells agents. It does not say how anything is built. It names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or interface delegation.\n\nI looked for an alternative that keeps the same agent-facing guidance but violates a structural clause. I found none. Any wording or section placement that gives the same direction satisfies the text. So there is no structural obligation that needs `{impl}` evidence.\n\nThe root `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md` does carry the directive. In the section `Proposing requirements`, it says: \"When a convention applies on its own at many sites, such as how every validation function continues after a failed check, propose a distributed requirement instead of marking each site `{impl}`; read the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`.\" This is a check of the behavior, not a structural finding.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one behavioral obligation. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention, rather than marking each site `{impl}`. The requirement is about the skill's text, so a text-content test is the right kind of evidence.\n\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_proposes_distributed_requirements_for_conventions]` takes the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` `SKILL.md` and extracts its `## Proposing requirements` section with `section`. That helper panics if the heading is missing. The test then uses `assert_text` to check that the section contains \"propose a distributed requirement instead of marking each site `{impl}`\". `assert_text` uses `contains`, so a missing or reworded directive fails the test. The phrase covers both halves of the obligation. The check is scoped to the right skill and section, so text from another skill cannot satisfy it.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it]` also carries a `{test}` marker for this requirement. It checks that the bootstrap skill refers to the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`, and that no skill restates another skill's topic. This supports the directive and is not strictly needed.\n\nThe requirement has no boundary or failure cases beyond the presence of the directive. The tests would fail if the skill dropped or reworded the guidance. I found no gap.",
